      <li style="--step:2"><span class="guide-overview-number">03</span><span>Keep the helpers you like</span></li>
    </ol>
    <p class="guide-overview-footer">Quick controls in the popup. The full editor in a tab.</p>
  </div>

  <section id="install" class="guide-section">
    <h2>Install Glaze.</h2>
    <p><a href={store}>Open Glaze in the Chrome Web Store</a> and choose <strong>Add to Chrome</strong>. Then pin it from Chrome’s puzzle-piece menu. No Glaze account needed.</p>
    <aside class="guide-callout">
      <strong>This guide follows Glaze 3.3.0.</strong>
      <p>The Chrome Web Store version may differ. Check the version shown there; use the GitHub package below for the 3.3.0 features in this guide.</p>
    </aside>
    <p><strong>New in 3.3.0:</strong> Canvas and Blackboard Ultra share a resizable sidebar: <strong>This page</strong> keeps the page’s own tools above the <strong>My widgets</strong> dock. It makes room beside your work on wide screens and opens as a drawer on narrow ones. Better To Do moves there and follows the current course, with an <strong>All courses</strong> option. Move widgets between the sidebar, Dashboard and floating.</p>
    <p>For an unpacked copy of Glaze 3.3.0:</p>
    <ol class="guide-steps">
      <li>Download <a href={`${source}/releases/download/v3.3.0/glaze-v3.3.0.zip`}>glaze-v3.3.0.zip</a> and extract it to a folder you’ll keep.</li>
      <li>Open <code>chrome://extensions</code>, turn on <strong>Developer mode</strong>, choose <strong>Load unpacked</strong> and select that folder.</li>
      <li>Disable any other Glaze copy, so only one runs.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>An unpacked copy doesn’t update itself. To switch to a store copy that includes these features, first use <strong>Export settings</strong> and Notes <strong>Export all</strong> to save both backups. Remove the unpacked copy, install the store copy, then import both backups. Keep them private: they include share keys. Glaze needs Chrome 113 or newer. On a Mac with Safari, follow the <a href="/guides/glaze-safari">Safari guide</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="firefox" class="guide-section">
    <h2>Try the Firefox preview</h2>
    <p>The latest Firefox package is Glaze 3.3.0 for <strong>desktop Firefox 140 or newer</strong>. This is a temporary development installation; it is not an Add-ons store installation. Mozilla explains the difference in its <a href="https://extensionworkshop.com/documentation/develop/temporary-installation-in-firefox/">temporary-installation guide</a>.</p>
    <ol class="guide-steps">
      <li>Download <a href={`${source}/releases/download/v3.3.0/glaze-v3.3.0-firefox.zip`}>glaze-v3.3.0-firefox.zip</a>.</li>
      <li>Open <code>about:debugging#/runtime/this-firefox</code>, choose <strong>Load Temporary Add-on…</strong> and select the ZIP.</li>
      <li>Open your school’s Blackboard Ultra, Canvas or Moodle site and reload. Check Glaze’s site access if it does not appear.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>Firefox removes temporary add-ons when it quits, so load the package again for a later session. Back up settings and notes before removing or changing copies; do not rely on this temporary installation as your only copy of study data. Google Calendar sync is not included. Local <strong>Export calendar (.ics)</strong> is available.</p>
    <p>The theme, surfaces, pets and school-specific sections below apply to this package too. The <code>chrome://extensions</code> instructions elsewhere in this guide apply to Chrome.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="ultra" class="guide-section">
    <h2>Check your Blackboard page.</h2>
    <p>Sign in to Blackboard as usual. Glaze works on any school’s Ultra pages, those whose address contains <code>/ultra/</code>, with nothing to configure. Login pages and Classic Blackboard keep their own look.</p>
    <p>If nothing changes: check Glaze is enabled on <code>chrome://extensions</code>, refresh tabs that were open before you installed it, and make sure the popup’s theme switch is on.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="popup" class="guide-section">
    <h2>Start with the popup.</h2>
    <p>Click Glaze for the quick controls, laid out as tiles. Changes apply to open Blackboard tabs at once.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Theme</strong> switches Glaze on and off. Your settings stay, so you can compare with Blackboard’s own look.</li>
      <li><strong>Look</strong> shows the one you’re wearing; click it for all ten. A Look is a palette, fonts, shape and background chosen to go together: Aurora Night, Library, Terminal, Lava Lounge, Synthwave, Clear, Lagoon, Rose Garden, Graveyard or Pumpkin Patch.</li>
      <li><strong>Due this week</strong> counts what’s left in the next seven days and lists the next three. Point at one for its full title, course and due time.</li>
      <li><strong>Palette</strong> has six: Frost, Midnight, Forest, Ember, Paper and Rose. Picking one turns off automatic light/dark switching.</li>
      <li><strong>Motion</strong> steps through Off, Subtle and Expressive.</li>
      <li><strong>Open the editor</strong> has everything else. A dot beside it means a new version has notes for you.</li>
    </ul>
    <figure class="guide-figure guide-figure-compact">
      <a href="/projects/glaze-popup.webp" aria-label="Open the full-size Glaze popup capture"><img src="/projects/glaze-popup.webp" alt="Glaze popup tiles: Theme on, the Frost look, three items due this week, palette swatches, Subtle motion and Open the editor" width="720" height="962" loading="lazy" /></a>
      <figcaption>The Glaze popup.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p>The due list uses Glaze’s cached course and planner reads. Open your school’s site to let it refresh. Nothing pops up and the toolbar icon gets no badge. If the tile shows a dash, open any Blackboard page first.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="palette" class="guide-section">
    <h2>Make a palette your own.</h2>
    <p>The editor has eight tabs: Look, Blackboard, Canvas, Moodle, Study tools, Sharing and sync, Backup and privacy, and About. It saves as you go. The sticky live preview switches between fictional LMS pages, light or dark mode, and desktop or phone widths. Section resets change only that section’s preferences. Type in <strong>Search settings</strong> at the top (or press <kbd>/</kbd>) to find one.</p>
    <p>Under <strong>Palette</strong>, change any of the fourteen colors and watch the contrast checks. Name it and choose <strong>Save as preset</strong> to keep it (up to twelve). Turn on <strong>Follow the system’s light and dark mode</strong> to switch between a light and a dark palette with your computer.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="look" class="guide-section">
    <h2>Fonts and reading.</h2>
    <p>Under <strong>Look → Fonts and shape</strong>, pick from about forty, each shown in its own face: reading faces such as Lexend, OpenDyslexic and Atkinson Hyperlegible Next, sans, serif and monospace families, and four Nerd Fonts. You can also type any Google Font’s name, or a font installed on your computer. Headings and code can have their own font. Opening the font list loads small previews from Google Fonts; choosing a web font downloads its full font files.</p>
    <figure class="guide-figure">
      <a href="/projects/glaze-editor-3.0.0.webp" aria-label="Open the full-size Glaze editor capture"><img src="/projects/glaze-editor-3.0.0.webp" alt="Glaze editor with palette and look controls beside its live sample course preview" width="1440" height="1000" loading="lazy" /></a>
      <figcaption>The editor and its sample course.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p>Raise <strong>Text size</strong> and <strong>Line height</strong> if reading feels cramped, and try <strong>Roomy</strong> density. <strong>Corner radius</strong>, <strong>Depth</strong> and <strong>Glass blur</strong> shape the panels. <strong>Hover hints</strong> show how far off a date is or a grade’s percentage.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="background" class="guide-section">
    <h2>Background and motion.</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Animated:</strong> ten slow scenes (Mist, Orbs, Borealis, Lava, Waves, Dust, Contours, Horizon, Graveyard, Pumpkin Patch), with one <strong>Intensity</strong> slider. They pause in background tabs and hold still when motion is off.</li>
      <li><strong>Gradient:</strong> linear, radial, conic or mesh, with angle, blend and spread.</li>
      <li><strong>Aurora</strong>, <strong>Solid</strong>, or <strong>Image</strong> from an <code>https://</code> address, with Dim and Blur.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Backgrounds use your palette’s colors, one of six ready-made sets, or three of your own.</p>
    <figure class="guide-figure">
      <a href="/projects/glaze-backgrounds-3.0.0.webp" aria-label="Open the full-size Glaze background editor capture"><img src="/projects/glaze-backgrounds-3.0.0.webp" alt="Glaze editor’s Background section with a mesh gradient beside the live sample course" width="1440" height="1000" loading="lazy" /></a>
      <figcaption>A mesh gradient in the Background section.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p><strong>Motion</strong> is Off, Subtle (default) or Expressive. Ultra page changes show immediately. Background scenes pause in hidden tabs; reduced motion keeps animations still. With <strong>Turn motion off when the system asks for reduced motion</strong> on, your system setting wins.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="surfaces" class="guide-section">
    <h2>Glass, frost and surfaces</h2>
    <p>Open <strong>Look → Surfaces</strong>, or search settings for “Surfaces”. These controls style the panels behind your content on Blackboard Ultra, Canvas and Moodle, including Glaze’s widgets, notes and menus.</p>
    <ol class="guide-steps">
      <li>Keep <strong>Surface opacity</strong> on <strong>Automatic</strong> to retain your Look’s original fills. For custom transparency, choose a picture, gradient or animated background and set <strong>Glass blur</strong> above zero. Solid backgrounds and zero blur keep custom surfaces opaque.</li>
      <li>Adjust <strong>Surface tint</strong> and choose <strong>Accent color</strong> or <strong>Course color</strong> as its source. Enable <strong>Use a custom surface color</strong> if you want to choose the fill yourself.</li>
      <li>Use <strong>Shadow strength</strong>, optional <strong>Shadow color</strong>, <strong>Surface frost</strong>, <strong>Border strength</strong> and <strong>Depth</strong> to make sections easier to distinguish. Corners follow <strong>Look → Fonts and shape → Corner radius</strong>.</li>
    </ol>
    <figure class="guide-figure">
      <a href="/projects/glaze-surfaces-3.0.2.webp" aria-label="Open the full-size Glaze surface controls capture"><img src="/projects/glaze-surfaces-3.0.2.webp" alt="Look → Surfaces in the released Glaze 3.0.2 editor, with opacity, tint, frost and shadow controls beside its fictional Blackboard preview" width="1440" height="1000" loading="lazy" /></a>
      <figcaption>The shared Surfaces controls, captured in Glaze 3.0.2 with sample courses.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p>Switch the live preview between sites and pages to check readability. Each control has <strong>Reset</strong>; <strong>Reset section</strong> restores the surface defaults. Small content rows use a fill without extra backdrop blur.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="pets" class="guide-section">
    <h2>Choose a pet</h2>
    <p>On Canvas or Blackboard Ultra, turn on <strong>Study tools → Pet habitat</strong> in the editor. Back on your school page, open <strong>Pet habitat</strong> from the command palette or <strong>What Glaze can do</strong>, then use <strong>Pet character</strong> to choose Ember the fox, Luna the owl, Mochi the cat, Sprout the dragon, Pebble the capybara, Rime the arctic fox or the Glaze droplet, or keep the Classic buddy.</p>
    <p>To import a pet you already have, return to <strong>Study tools → Pet habitat</strong> in the editor and use <strong>Import a pet package</strong>. Choose its <code>pet.json</code> and PNG or WebP sprite sheet together, a ZIP containing them, or the sprite sheet alone. ChatGPT v1 and v2 layouts are supported. Files stay on this device; no account connection or upload is involved. Importing a character keeps your existing XP and decorations.</p>
    <figure class="guide-figure">
      <a href="/projects/glaze-pets-3.0.2.webp" aria-label="Open the full-size Glaze pet library capture"><img src="/projects/glaze-pets-3.0.2.webp" alt="Glaze 3.0.2’s Pet habitat settings and local import control, with Ember the fox, Luna the owl and Mochi the cat in the animation library" width="1440" height="1000" loading="lazy" /></a>
      <figcaption>Import and preview pets in the editor; choose your character in the habitat on your school page.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p>Click an animated pet to wave, or focus it and press <kbd>Enter</kbd> or <kbd>Space</kbd> to jump. Animation pauses when hidden or offscreen. Reduced motion and Glaze Motion off show a still frame.</p>
    <p>The habitat earns XP from stored handed-in work, kept “do on” plans and verified streak days when Streak is enabled. Levels continue across semesters; settings backups carry progress, furniture and learned preferences. Focus sessions and petting add no XP. There are no notifications or missed-day penalties. Progress and decorations belong to your signed-in school account.</p>
    <p>Choose a room, moss garden, water tank or cloud loft, then place furniture and ball or yarn toys from the tray. Drag a toy onto the page to invite a short play session, or let your companion settle on a favorite perch. Typing, scrolling and focus keep it calm.</p>
    <p><strong>Learn from how I play</strong> adapts routines and toy preferences from bounded numeric signals such as petting, play and time of day. Six meters show mood, playfulness, energy, bond, curiosity and comfort. The model stays in this account’s local pet record and chosen backups; typed text and page content are not training data, and no cloud model is used. Turn learning off or use <strong>Reset learned behavior</strong> without changing XP.</p>
    <aside class="guide-callout"><strong>New in 3.0.4: mods.</strong><p>Add backgrounds (shaders, layered scenes, videos, animated pictures, Lottie), motion and event effects, nav layouts, page tweaks, and sticker and pet packs and widgets made by others, from a code, link, file or the gallery's Mods section. Each mod shows what it may read, and why, before you add it. Code mods run sandboxed. Updates are signed, with an opt-out signed blocklist, and a no-code widget builder makes your own. Looks can also use a looping video, animated WebP/AVIF or Lottie background: muted, still when motion is off, paused in hidden tabs. See the <a href="/guides/glaze-mods-api">mods guide</a>.</p></aside>
  </section>

  <section id="courses" class="guide-section">
    <h2>Course colors and tint.</h2>
    <p>Glaze softens Ultra’s course colors to fit your palette. Every course you haven’t hidden appears in the editor’s <strong>Courses</strong> section once you’ve opened Blackboard, organizations listed apart. Blackboard has only ten course colors; Glaze gives each course its own, and you can change it. A calendar entry takes its course’s color when its title belongs to one course.</p>
    <p>Each course also has a <strong>Look</strong> menu: keep your theme, or pick one of the ten Looks for that course. Inside that course Glaze wears it; everywhere else keeps your theme.</p>
    <p>Turn on <strong>Tint the theme inside a course</strong> and tick courses: inside each, the accent and background take that course’s color, so you can tell where you are at a glance.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="extras" class="guide-section">
    <h2>Helpers.</h2>
    <p>Find each helper in its site tab or Study tools; every helper can be switched off.</p>
    <div class="guide-table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">On by default</th><th scope="col">What it does</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Due-date status</td><td>“in 2 days · Unopened”, “Submitted”, “Submitted late”, “Graded · 18/20”, and grade percentages.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Grades on course cards</td><td>Your overall grade on the Courses page, where your instructor shares it.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>New-grade and new-content badges</td><td>“New” beside grades and items added since your last visit, with counts on course cards.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>What-if calculator</td><td>On a course’s gradebook: the overall grade your scores would make, or the score you’d need. Your scores stay until you choose <strong>Clear scores</strong>, and each goes once that item gets a real grade.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Remember your place</td><td>Back in a course, even after a reload, the folders you had open reopen and it scrolls to where you were. Folders you close stay closed, and scrolling yourself always wins.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Command palette</td><td><kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>K</kbd> (<kbd>⌘K</kbd> on a Mac) jumps to a course, item, page or setting, and lists what’s due this week. Type “settings” to open the editor.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Download all</td><td>A folder’s files as one zip. Items you haven’t opened are left out, so nothing gets marked as viewed.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Back up what you type</td><td>If a page reloads or times out before you post, Glaze offers your text back.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Folder previews, course progress, score bars</td><td>A folder’s contents on hover, a course’s completion at the top of its Content page, and a bar under each score.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Document buttons</td><td>Focus mode for course documents, and the dark-documents switch.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>Off until you want them: due-date urgency, shorter descriptions, reading width, keyboard shortcuts (<kbd>?</kbd> lists them), dark documents (dark PDFs on dark palettes), and <strong>Declutter</strong> for page parts you don’t use.</p>
    <p>Glaze follows your school site’s language: English, Spanish, French, German, Portuguese (Brazil) or Dutch.</p>
    <aside class="guide-callout"><strong>Blackboard remains the record.</strong><p>Check due dates and scores against Blackboard before relying on them. If a helper looks wrong, switch it off and tell us.</p></aside>
  </section>

  <section id="workspace" class="guide-section">
    <h2>Your Ultra workspace</h2>
    <p>Open <strong>Dashboard</strong> in Ultra’s rail for Glaze’s board; the <strong>Institution page</strong> tab keeps your school’s page reachable. On Courses, use the Glaze toolbar’s <strong>Edit layout</strong>. Add, move, resize, fold or remove tiles. <strong>+ Course cards</strong> replaces the native course list with individual cards grouped by term; remove the course tiles to bring the native list back. The Dashboard can add individual courses too.</p>
    <p><strong>Better To Do</strong> is a tile on both boards, with your due work and your own tasks, steps, plans and focus sessions. Ultra ticks stay on this device. Reminders, streaks and Insights are optional; check your school’s own dates and grades when something looks incomplete.</p>
    <p><strong>Course tabs</strong> keep up to four additional course pages live alongside the page you started on. Hidden pages no longer close after ten idle minutes, including on smaller computers. When the four-page limit is reached, an older unedited page may reopen at its saved URL and scroll; when all four have been edited, save and close one before opening another. These sit inside Ultra rather than being browser tabs: the address bar and browser Back are not a separate history for each one. Save your work before closing a course tab, reloading, or turning course tabs or Glaze off. Glaze cannot guarantee that every native operation or unsaved change will survive.</p>
    <p>Use <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>K</kbd> (<kbd>⌘K</kbd> on a Mac) for course search and tools. On an outline, a folder’s eye hides it only for you. <strong>Show hidden folders</strong> reveals dimmed folders and their unhide controls; the brief action message offers <strong>Undo</strong>.</p>
    <p>In the editor’s <strong>Blackboard</strong> tab, customize the navigation’s links, order, icons, widths and text. A collapsed rail can expand on hover or keyboard focus without moving the page. Turn Dashboard off there to restore Ultra’s Institution Page label and icon.</p>
    <p>In <strong>Study tools</strong>, opt into <strong>Notes</strong> for rich text, pictures and attachments, or <strong>Pet habitat</strong> for a buddy that earns decorations from stored hand-ins and kept plans. The pet starts off, never nags, and doesn’t penalize missed days.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="canvas" class="guide-section">
    <h2>Use Glaze on Canvas</h2>
    <p>Glaze recognizes Canvas on <code>instructure.com</code> and your school’s own domain. Allow Glaze on that site and reload. The same themes apply, with helpers where Canvas keeps your work:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Dashboard:</strong> Better To Do and optional notes, agenda, focus timer and study-buddy widgets. Arrange the widgets with <strong>Edit widgets</strong>.</li>
      <li><strong>Courses:</strong> search inside course content; module previews, new-item badges, your place, Download all and progress from completion requirements. Download all leaves unopened files out.</li>
      <li><strong>Grades:</strong> Glaze’s what-if calculator uses Canvas’s grading rules; an unmatched total is labelled as an estimate. Your real grades stay unchanged.</li>
      <li><strong>Study tools:</strong> review an imported class schedule, syllabus or past GPA terms before saving. PDF and photo text extraction happens on your device; correct the result before using it.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Better To Do ticks stay local unless you choose <strong>Tick off in Canvas too</strong>. That option writes only your planner’s done state, never a submission or a grade. Local calendar-file export needs no Google connection.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="moodle" class="guide-section">
    <h2>Use Glaze on Moodle</h2>
    <p>Open your school’s Moodle site, allow Glaze there and reload. Glaze recognizes Boost pages on Moodle 5 and compatible Moodle 4.x markup: Dashboard, My courses, course and activity pages, grades, calendar and messages.</p>
    <p>The same Looks, palettes, fonts, backgrounds and motion apply. Course cards can show the total Moodle makes available. <strong>Due this week</strong> reads your Timeline; the command palette lists courses and Moodle pages. Set a course’s tint and Look from its card menu or the editor. Optional keyboard shortcuts pause while you type or take a quiz.</p>
    <p>Moodle’s helpers are distinct: the Ultra live tabs, Dashboard board, rich notes and pet habitat described above are not Moodle features.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="backup" class="guide-section">
    <h2>Share, back up, privacy.</h2>
    <p><strong>Share sessions</strong> is separate from theme codes and starts disconnected. Create or join an invitation to share selected notes and tasks with end-to-end encryption. The invitation contains its key: keep it private. You can choose a custom relay for new sessions; existing sessions keep their original server. A link on another server asks for confirmation before joining. See <a href={`${source}/blob/main/server/README.md`}>Run your own relay</a>.</p>
    <p>Settings backups and Notes <strong>Export all</strong> include share keys and creator delete tokens, even while sharing is paused. Notes Export all opens a confirmation on Glaze’s own extension page; settings backups are made in the extension editor. Anyone with that backup may open, edit or delete those sessions, so keep it private. Importing keys stays offline until you explicitly resume.</p>

    <p>In <strong>Look → Palette and Looks</strong>, <strong>Copy theme code</strong> gives your palette, fonts, look and background as one line of text. Paste someone’s code, check it with <strong>Preview</strong>, then choose <strong>Use this theme</strong> or <strong>Cancel</strong>.</p>
    <p><strong>Export settings</strong> saves your preferences, card setup, Better To Do data, hidden-folder choices and share keys to <code>glaze-settings.json</code>; <strong>Import settings…</strong> restores a file’s setup. Use Notes <strong>Export all</strong> separately for rich notes and their attachments. <strong>Reset everything</strong> needs a second click and keeps your saved palettes. Settings also follow your Chrome profile when sync is on.</p>
    <p>Glaze has no analytics and needs no account or server for local tools. Notes, tasks, what-if scores and course history stay in extension storage unless you explicitly share or export them. Read-only requests use your existing school session; an optional Canvas planner tick is the exception described above. Chosen web fonts and background images load from their source. Sharing sends encrypted content and connection metadata to the relay you choose. Invite links and backups are sensitive. Glaze never submits coursework. Details are in the <a href="/glaze/privacy">privacy policy</a> and <a href="/glaze/terms">terms</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="recovery" class="guide-section">
    <h2>Fix a problem.</h2>
    <ul class="guide-checklist">
      <li><strong>Features from this guide missing:</strong> check the version on <code>chrome://extensions</code>. For Glaze 3.3.0, use the GitHub package above.</li>
      <li><strong>Courses missing in the editor:</strong> open any Blackboard page with Glaze on, then reopen the editor.</li>
      <li><strong>Popup works, Blackboard doesn’t change:</strong> check the theme switch and the <code>/ultra/</code> address, then refresh the tab.</li>
      <li><strong>A Blackboard file preview is blank:</strong> Glaze 3.0.4 refreshes expired viewer tickets for the same document and retries previews while an uploaded file converts, for up to five minutes. Save any unsaved work before manually reloading the page. Glaze does not resubmit the file.</li>
      <li><strong>One course looks different:</strong> it has its own Look. Set it back to your theme under Courses.</li>
      <li><strong>Due this week shows a dash:</strong> open any Blackboard page, then the popup again.</li>
      <li><strong>Palette keeps switching:</strong> automatic light/dark mode is on. Turn it off in Palette.</li>
      <li><strong>Background is still:</strong> motion is Off or your system asks for reduced motion. Without WebGL, use a gradient.</li>
      <li><strong>A page is hard to use:</strong> switch Glaze off to compare, go back to a built-in palette, and turn off the last helper you changed.</li>
      <li><strong>“Not saved”:</strong> keep the editor open and read the error. Use <strong>Export settings</strong> and Notes <strong>Export all</strong> before uninstalling.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>When reporting an issue, include your Chrome and Glaze versions and the page type, and hide names and grades in screenshots.</p>
